---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import ProjectHero from '../../components/portfolio/ProjectHero.astro';
import ResponsiveImage from '../../components/ResponsiveImage.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Between Strangers"
  subtitle="Reconstructing subtle embodied social experience as designable relations before symbolic-form selection."
  description="An empirical-to-design methodology project demonstrated through public-space gaze, first-person inquiry, image schemas and an XR-oriented design proposition."
  period="2025–Present"
  kind="Empirical-to-Design Methodology"
  status="Design proposition · evaluation pending"
  projectClass="portfolio-page--research portfolio-page--narrative"
  nextHref="/work/minetrarchi"
  nextTitle="Minetrarchi"
>
  <ProjectHero class="project-hero--research">
    <ResponsiveImage
      src="/pictures/research_white.png"
      alt="A symbolic point suspended in a light field"
      priority
    />
    <div class="project-hero__inner">
      <p>
        An underground corridor. A stranger’s gaze lands on you for two seconds. What just
        happened—and what could a design say about it?
      </p>
    </div>
  </ProjectHero>

  <section class="case-section case-opening">
    <p class="case-label">The design problem</p>
    <h2>A Glance That Leaves No Trace</h2>
    <p class="case-lead">
      Public-space gaze is often brief, backgrounded, embedded in walking, and difficult to describe
      as a stable event.
    </p>
    <div class="case-opening__copy">
      <p class="case-copy">
        Yet it can reorganize attention, interpersonal boundaries, and possible interaction.
        Something may have happened—it simply left no legible trace.
      </p>
      <p class="case-copy">Two ways to fail frame the problem.</p>
      <p class="case-copy">
        One is to draw the meaning: a force striking a body, a spotlight on a person—images that
        turn an interpretation into a verdict.
      </p>
      <p class="case-copy">
        The other is to stop at findings that describe the experience but never say what to do with
        it.
      </p>
      <p class="case-copy">Both fail at the same point: they skip the relations.</p>
      <p class="case-copy">
        The challenge was not to invent a symbol for “being looked at.” It was to find out which
        parts of this subtle experience could be reconstructed as relations a designer could work
        with, before deciding how those relations should look or behave.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="state-strip__lead">Four states a design must not conflate.</p>
    <div class="state-strip" aria-label="Four states that must remain distinct">
      <article>
        <small>01 · Behavior</small><h3>A gaze occurred</h3><p>
          Someone’s eyes were directed toward you in the world.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>02 · Perception</small><h3>A gaze was noticed</h3><p>
          It entered your awareness as a cue, distinct from the background.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>03 · Relation</small><h3>An exchange formed</h3><p>
          You looked back, and two gazes met in a shared relation.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>04 · Meaning</small><h3>A meaning was construed</h3><p>
          It came to mean something to someone—invitation, scrutiny, threat, or nothing at all.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="state-strip__note">
      Keeping these states distinct preserves room for interpretation. Collapsing them risks turning
      inference into judgment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section case-fieldwork">
    <p class="case-label">Entering the experience</p>
    <h2>A Walk, Then a Conversation</h2>
    <div class="research-numbers case-numbers">
      <div><strong>16</strong><span>native Mandarin-speaking participants</span></div>
      <div><strong>13:02</strong><span>hours of interviews</span></div>
      <div><strong>160,162</strong><span>Chinese characters transcribed</span></div>
      <div><strong>40–60</strong><span>minutes per session</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="case-action-sequence">
      <article>
        <strong>Watch</strong><p>
          Participants watched a first-person, five-minute walk through an underground pedestrian
          passage in urban Shanghai.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>Recall</strong><p>
          They returned to moments when another person, a face, or a gaze entered attention—and to
          comparable episodes from everyday life.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>Describe</strong><p>
          The interview followed what was noticed, what remained in the background, what changed
          after noticing, and how the episode ended.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>Trace</strong><p>
          Every evidence unit received a unique ID, so later design decisions could be followed back
          to the account that supported them.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="case-boundary">
      The gaze direction of people in the video was not measured. The material consists of situated
      descriptions, not recordings of real-time cognition.
    </p>

    <h3 class="case-subheading">Two Different Jobs in the Design Process</h3>
    <div class="case-result-pair">
      <article>
        <p class="case-result-pair__method">Perceptual analysis</p>
        <h3>What allowed gaze to enter—or leave—experience?</h3>
        <p>
          Walking and collision avoidance often stayed primary. A person could become noticeable
          before their gaze was localized.
        </p>
        <p>
          Participants associated noticeability with field of view, environmental distraction,
          passage pressure, duration, and persistence. Looking away or passing could close the
          episode and return attention to walking.
        </p>
        <strong
          >These findings were translated into conditional requirements: make a gaze event available
          without demanding constant monitoring; distinguish a visible person from a gaze directed
          toward self; show whether the relation is brief, continuing, or over; and make withdrawal
          and return to baseline perceptible.</strong
        >
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="case-result-pair__method">Conceptual-metaphor analysis</p>
        <h3>What relations organized participants’ descriptions?</h3>
        <p>
          Across different accounts, gaze was described as something that travelled, arrived,
          stayed, or met another gaze.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also carried content, exerted pressure, crossed a boundary, illuminated a person, or
          exposed a watcher.
        </p>
        <strong
          >These expressions were not treated as ready-made design ideas. Each metaphorical mapping
          was reconstructed into its underlying spatial and event relations, then checked against
          its evidence record.</strong
        >
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-turn">
    <div class="case-turn__inner">
      <p class="case-label">The critical turn</p>
      <h2>The Interviews Did Not Tell Me What to Draw</h2>
      <p class="case-turn__lead">
        The two analyses defined functions and relations, not a visual form.
      </p>
      <p class="case-copy">
        A participant might describe a prolonged gaze as pressure or intrusion. Drawing pressure
        striking a body would preserve that particular construal—but it would turn a situated
        interpretation into a judgment made by the system.
      </p>
      <p class="case-copy">
        Literal translation would therefore preserve the participant’s words while losing the
        distinction between an account of experience and a claim made by the system.
      </p>
      <div class="case-example">
        <article>
          <small>Situated account</small><strong
            >Prolonged gaze was sometimes described through pressure.</strong
          >
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>Literal translation</small><strong
            >A force could be shown acting directly on the person.</strong
          >
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>Why it was rejected</small><strong
            >That image would present pressure, threat, and an expected response as facts rather
            than possible interpretations.</strong
          >
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="case-turn__close">
        The interviews gave the design relations, not images. That was the contribution.
      </p>
      <p class="case-copy">
        The question became what to do with those relations—which to keep, which to move, which to
        refuse.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section case-translation">
    <p class="case-label">The evidence</p>
    <h2>What the Evidence Could Support</h2>
    <p class="case-lead">
      For every audited metaphorical mapping, I separated the participant’s situated meaning from
      the more basic spatial or event relation that organized it.
    </p>
    <p class="case-copy">Compatible relations were then synthesized across records.</p>
    <p class="case-copy">
      The result describes what the evidence supports—not what I wanted the final imagery to look
      like.
    </p>
    <div
      class="evidence-story"
      aria-label="Concrete reading of the Evidence-Side Image-Schematic System"
    >
      <article>
        <small>Participants described gaze as moving, arriving, staying, or leaving.</small><h3>
          This supports a directed path with an origin, movement, arrival, persistence, withdrawal,
          and restoration.
        </h3>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>They described two gazes as meeting, holding, breaking, or meeting again.</small><h3>
          This supports contact formation, maintenance, separation, and possible recontact between
          two directed gaze relations.
        </h3>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small
          >Some accounts described private activity as a bounded space and prolonged, nonreciprocal
          gaze as entering it.</small
        ><h3>
          This supports a conditional bounded-access branch—not a claim that every sustained gaze is
          intrusion.
        </h3>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small
          >Other accounts gave gaze weight, depth, sharpness, pressure, content, illumination, or
          the capacity to expose a watcher.</small
        ><h3>
          These become attached qualities or conditional modules. They modify the backbone and do
          not form a universal event sequence.
        </h3>
      </article>
    </div>
    <figure class="evidence-figure">
      <ResponsiveImage
        src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/evidenceside.png"
        alt="Evidence-Side Image-Schematic System: the directed-path and contact backbone, the conditional bounded-access branch, and the attached quality, visibility, and detection modules"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The directed-path and contact backbone, the conditional bounded-access branch, and the
        attached quality, visibility, and detection modules—each linked to its evidence records.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="case-system-name">
      <span>Output of the metaphor branch</span><strong>Evidence-Side Image-Schematic System</strong
      ><em
        >A cross-record synthesis of evidence-supported relations. Every component remains traceable
        to its source mapping, and each individual record instantiates only part of the system.</em
      >
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section case-projection">
    <div class="case-projection__inner">
      <p class="case-label">The design decisions</p>
      <h2>Keep, Move, Refuse</h2>
      <p class="case-lead">
        The evidence gave me relations, not instructions. Selective projection decided what to do
        with them—what to keep, what to move, what to constrain, and what to refuse.
      </p>
      <p class="case-copy">
        Four inputs met in the projection: the evidence-side relations, the perceptual functions,
        the design value, and the reference situation.
      </p>
      <p class="case-copy">
        Keeping meaning with the people is not a slogan here—it became a set of concrete decisions
        about what the system would never show.
      </p>
      <div class="projection-inputs projection-inputs--compact">
        <article>
          <small>From perceptual findings</small><p>
            Let gaze emerge from the walking background; locate its source and direction;
            distinguish brief, continuing, and ended relations; preserve withdrawal and return to
            baseline.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>From metaphorical mappings</small><p>
            Path, contact, boundedness, content, pressure, illumination, and detection were
            available as evidence-supported relational resources—not mandatory visual features.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>From the design value</small><p>
            Support gaze exchange as a recognizable, optional entry point into social interaction.
            Recognition must not become a demand to respond.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>From the reference situation</small><p>
            The imagery must coexist with walking, route maintenance, passing strangers, short
            encounters, and each participant’s first-person viewpoint.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="projection-decisions">
        <article>
          <small>Retain</small><h3>Gaze as directed movement, contact, and withdrawal</h3><p>
            The object shows source, direction, arrival, persistence, and withdrawal.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>Relocate</small><h3>Arrival moves to the space between, not the body</h3><p>
            Endpoints leave the person; the object stops at a common relation position between the
            two people.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>Constrain</small><h3>Pressure and intrusion become limits, not states</h3><p>
            Objects stay outside both domains; sharpness stays low; no force acts on the person.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="projection-restraints">
        <article>
          <small>Restraint</small><h3>Interior without content</h3><p>
            Objects keep a container interior; the system never fills, labels, or classifies it.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>Restraint</small><h3>No spotlight</h3><p>
            Illumination and exposure are excluded; event changes appear in the relation, not on the
            person.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>Restraint</small><h3>Geometry without judgment</h3><p>
            “Catching” is reduced to contact geometry; reciprocity shows without accountability or
            blame.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section case-system">
    <p class="case-label">One relation system, three carriers</p>
    <h2>One Relation System, Three Visual Carriers</h2>
    <p class="case-lead">
      Only after the relations had been selected and reorganized could the design-side system be
      specified. It fixes the sequence below while leaving shape, material, color, and animation
      open.
    </p>
    <div
      class="design-sequence"
      aria-label="Design-side state sequence: baseline, gaze formation, return gaze, contact, withdrawal, and restoration"
    >
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/designside1.png"
          alt="Baseline: only the two participant domains remain"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Baseline—only the two domains remain. No gaze object is visible.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/designside2.png"
          alt="A gaze forms: an object appears at a boundary and travels between"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          A gaze forms—an object appears at a boundary and travels toward the space between.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/designside3.png"
          alt="Return gaze: a second object appears from the opposite side"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Return gaze—a second object appears from the opposite side.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/designside4.png"
          alt="Contact: the two objects merge into one relation object"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Contact—the two objects merge. Contact is present, nothing more.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/designside5.png"
          alt="Withdrawal: the object splits and the withdrawing one returns"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Withdrawal—the object splits; the withdrawing one returns and disappears.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/designside1.png"
          alt="Restored: the imagery returns to its baseline"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Restored—all objects vanish; the imagery returns to its baseline.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="case-system-name">
      <span>Output of selective projection</span><strong>Design-Side Image-Schematic System</strong
      ><em
        >A carrier-independent behavior specification derived from the evidence-side relations,
        conditional perceptual requirements, design value, and reference situation.</em
      >
    </p>
    <p class="representational-boundary">
      The design-side system represents observable gaze relations and state changes, while specific
      meaning construal remains participant-level.
    </p>

    <h3 class="case-subheading">Three Possible Carriers</h3>
    <div class="carrier-rows">
      <figure class="carrier-row">
        <div class="carrier-row__visual">
          <ResponsiveImage
            src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/carrier-membrane.png"
            alt="Membrane–Bubble visual carrier: transparent domains and bubble-like gaze objects"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="carrier-row__panel">
          <h3>Membrane–Bubble</h3>
          <p>
            Transparent domains and bubble-like objects make generation, movement, merge, split, and
            reabsorption physically continuous.
          </p>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="carrier-row">
        <div class="carrier-row__visual">
          <ResponsiveImage
            src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/carrier-wireframe.png"
            alt="Wireframe–Ring visual carrier: outlined domains and hollow ring gaze objects"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="carrier-row__panel">
          <h3>Wireframe–Ring</h3>
          <p>
            Low-occlusion outlines reduce visual mass, but their orientation and state changes
            invite competing spatial readings.
          </p>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="carrier-row">
        <div class="carrier-row__visual">
          <ResponsiveImage
            src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/carrier-field.png"
            alt="Field–Condensation visual carrier: particle-field domains and condensing gaze objects"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="carrier-row__panel">
          <h3>Field–Condensation</h3>
          <p>
            Particles make appearance and disappearance natural, but the boundary and object roles
            become harder to distinguish.
          </p>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="carrier-rows__note">
      The same participant domains, gaze objects, common relation position, and merge/split behavior
      were expressed through three different visual languages.
    </p>
    <p class="case-review">
      Five external designers with graduate-level interaction-design education and experience in XR
      imagery compared the candidates.
    </p>
    <p class="case-review">
      Their review supported Membrane–Bubble for further development—it did not validate the method,
      behavior script, or experiential effect.
    </p>

    <h3 class="case-subheading">The Proposition: Membrane–Bubble</h3>
    <div class="membrane-gallery">
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/membrane-domain.png"
          alt="A transparent membrane surrounding a person in an underground passage"
          loading="lazy"
        /><figcaption>A gazer-side membrane establishes the visible domain.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/membrane-emergence.png"
          alt="A localized deformation forming on a transparent membrane"
          loading="lazy"
        /><figcaption>A local deformation marks where an event object begins.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/membrane-object.png"
          alt="A transparent bubble emerging from a membrane"
          loading="lazy"
        /><figcaption>
          The object separates from the boundary and moves toward the relation position.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/between-strangers/membrane-contact.png"
          alt="Two transparent bubbles merging into a reciprocal relation object"
          loading="lazy"
        /><figcaption>Two event objects merge when reciprocal gaze contact forms.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="case-current-form__note">
      These images specify a visual proposition, not a finished XR experience.
    </p>
    <p class="case-current-form__note">
      The complete transition sequence, sensing implementation, visual load while walking, situated
      interpretation, and social consequences still require prototyping and evaluation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section case-status">
    <p class="case-label">What this produced</p>
    <h2>What the Project Actually Produced</h2>
    <div class="status-pair">
      <article>
        <h3>Produced</h3><p>
          A traceable way to move from first-person accounts to design requirements; an
          evidence-side reconstruction; a documented set of projection decisions; a design-side
          behavior script; three visual carriers; and a preferred proposition for further
          development.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Still open</h3><p>
          Whether gaze can be sensed reliably, whether the imagery remains legible without
          disturbing walking, how participants interpret and act on it, and what consequences it
          creates for people who did not choose to use the system.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="case-closing">
      The proposition visualizes observable relations and state changes—and leaves specific meaning
      construal with the people.
    </p>
    <p class="case-closing">
      The contribution is not a finished visualization of gaze. It is a method for reconstructing
      subtle embodied social experience as designable relations before symbolic form is chosen: a
      way to make an invisible relation legible without deciding in advance what it means.
    </p>
    <p class="case-closing">
      Demonstrated here through a proposition that can now be built, contested, and revised.
    </p>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
